From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 22:25:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] audio: keep the primary input's frames to real time

The primary input takes the frames its client asks for from a pipe that
an I/O thread fills with the buffers the device captures. When the pipe
was short it made silence up for the missing frames (a warning in the
log each time), and its sleep pacing made some up to keep a schedule of
its own. A recording thus held a gap of silence about that often, the
captured frames behind it came as late as the gap was long, and the
timestamps of the input could not be smooth.

The device paces the input, so the pacing of its own goes, with its
members. A read waits for its frames, and the I/O thread wakes the wait
each time it has written to the pipe; the wait ends when the thread is
stopped. The position of the input stays its client's count and the
time of the reply.

The count has to follow the time as well, because a recorder stamps what
it reads from the time of the read. When the device delivers nothing for
a while (the worker of the sound device blocked, a busy host), a read
that gave up after its bounded wait and made up only its own frames
left the count behind the time by the stall: a recording held less
audio than its timestamps spanned, a player that follows the audio
clock skipped ahead at every gap, and clips played back too fast.

So the frames of the client are due one after the other from a clock
that starts with the first transfer, and starts again when the client
was away for longer than the longest wait (a pause) or the count is
more than 5 s behind it. A read waits for its frames until 100 ms after
the last of them is due, which covers what the device delivers late in
the normal course, and makes the missing ones up then: the count
follows the time through a stall, and nothing is made up while the
device delivers. Frames that come when the count is already ahead of
the clock by more than that slack belong to time that was made up, and
are dropped, oldest first.
